Begin quantum descriptions with preparation transformations and observed outcomes (@physics-quantum-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Represent-a-pure-state-without-confusing-amplitudes-and-probabilities.lingua#r_00E559710A7C552718A18212FA") { r_00B05CC7CF19E5F22AADA0F422
Reuse [Review oscillations waves boundaries and physical evidence](Review-oscillations-waves-boundaries-and-physical-evidence.lingua#r_003B1CBD2375314B99BCD448E3), [Read electronic states and orbital pictures as models of evidence](../../chemistry/core/Read-electronic-states-and-orbital-pictures-as-models-of-evidence.lingua#r_05EBFD205C1399B2027FBE417B) and [Review finite complex geometry and evolution before assigning physical meaning](../../mathematics/core/Review-finite-complex-geometry-and-evolution-before-assigning-physical-meaning.lingua#r_00830E077A601AFC41FCE357A6). The wave lessons developed superposition for a classical displacement field; the chemistry lessons introduced spectra and orbital models. Neither alone supplies a quantum rule connecting a preparation with individual measurement outcomes. Begin by distinguishing the physical system, its preparation, controlled transformations, the measurement setting and the recorded result. Specify repetitions and exclusions before comparing an observed frequency with a theoretical probability.

The Stern–Gerlach observation described in [Quantum Physics II by Barton Zwiebach MIT 8 05](../sources/Quantum-Physics-II-by-Barton-Zwiebach-MIT-8-05.lingua#r_0093B354ED191AE21C5AAA51E0) concerns a beam of silver atoms passing through an inhomogeneous magnetic field and producing a split spatial pattern. Interpreting the deflection requires a model of magnetic moment, field and detection. It motivates a two-valued spin-component description for the selected degree of freedom, not a claim that each atom has only two possible positions or energies. Spin is intrinsic angular momentum; a classical rotating ball of charge is not its model. The magnetic apparatus details will connect to the remaining field/induction formation. Here an ideal analyzer is defined by the spin component it measures and the assumptions of its two-outcome account.

The same source then considers ideal sequences of spin analyzers. Those arrangements are teaching models, explicitly introduced as thought experiments; they are not raw historical data supplied for reanalysis. Preparing a definite z result, measuring along x, and returning to z illustrates a distinction between incompatible preparations. The new calculations will reconstruct the model's conditional probabilities while keeping rejected particles in the account. An actual experiment needs calibrated axes, preparation fidelity, detection efficiencies, timing and uncertainties.

The wavefunction discussion in [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../../engineering/materials-engineering/sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0) describes localized electron detections accumulating into an interference distribution. Individual events and an ensemble pattern are different observables. A classical continuous wave amplitude can reproduce some intensity patterns, but this does not make it a complete model of individually detected quantum systems. Equally, drawing two classical trajectories and adding their separate probabilities misses coherent cross terms under the stated preparation. Distinguish a mathematical prediction, a source's account of observations and a newly performed measurement.

**Read:** MIT note 2's introductory and analyzer passages, alongside Tong sections 1.1 and 1.3. The source notes delimit actual inspection and broader assignments. **Work:** write one observation question in the preparation–transformation–measurement language. State what would count as a recorded outcome, an exclusion and evidence against the ideal model. Name which quantities come from apparatus characterization and which are postulates of the quantum description. Do not infer a unique interpretation of quantum mechanics from this introductory evidence alone.

**Continue:** [Represent a pure state without confusing amplitudes and probabilities](Represent-a-pure-state-without-confusing-amplitudes-and-probabilities.lingua#r_00E559710A7C552718A18212FA).

